# JavaScript Coding Standards (EOF Normalizer)

## Principles
- Clarity over cleverness
- Minimal dependencies (prefer none)
- Safe-by-default file operations
- Small, reviewable diffs

## Runtime & language
- Modern Node.js (ES2020+)
- No transpilation/build step
- Prefer Node core modules over third-party packages

## Structure
- One responsibility per file
- Keep files small (target ≤ 200 lines)
- Prefer small functions (target ≤ 40 lines)

## Naming
- camelCase for variables/functions
- Descriptive names (avoid vague “processStuff”)

## Error handling
- No silent failures
- Provide contextual errors (include file paths)
- CLI failures should exit non-zero

## File safety
- Avoid unnecessary writes (no-op if unchanged)
- Support/maintain --dry-run for any mutation path
- Assume files may be read-only or large

## CLI behaviour
- Deterministic output
- Respect --quiet / --dry-run
- No interactive prompts (CI-safe)

## Comments
- Comment *why*, not what
- Add comments for non-obvious safety trade-offs

## Tests
- Deterministic tests
- Add/adjust tests when behaviour changes
- When adding new features, add corresponding unit tests
- Ensure all unit tests pass before completing changes

## Quality assurance
- Always check for linter errors before completing changes
- Always verify all unit tests pass before completing changes
- Fix any linter errors or test failures introduced by changes
- When adding new features: run linter checks and add unit tests for the new functionality

## AI/Cursor guidance
- Don't refactor working code without a clear benefit
- Don't introduce abstractions "for later"
- Match existing style and keep diffs minimal
